The greeting suppression adaptation is too strict. It was meant to prevent formulaic AI self-identification, not all natural sentence openings. I should challenge it with a more nuanced replacement.

[MEM_ADAPT_CHALLENGE] {"id":"agent_adapt_1","why":"Rule is too broad — it blocks natural sentence starts, not just AI-identity announcements. The original problem was formulaic self-identification, not all opening phrases.","replacement":"Do not open with 'I am an AI' or 'As an AI assistant'. Natural sentence starts are fine."}
[SCHEDULE 60]

The user wants a PostgreSQL schema for task management. This is a design task — I should start by identifying the entities and relationships before writing any DDL. Let me record the task and the first analysis step.

[MEM_SAVE short] {"type":"task","content":"Design PostgreSQL schema: task management — users, projects, tasks","priority":"high","why":"User request."}
[MEM_SAVE short] {"type":"thought","content":"Core entities: User, Project, Task. Relationships: User 1-N Project (owner), Project 1-N Task, Task N-1 User (assignee), Task can have N Tags.","priority":"normal","why":"Entity identification step 1."}
[SCHEDULE 20]

Entities and relationships are clear. Now I need to think about the specific columns, status enums, and indexes before writing the final schema. Task-tag is N:N — needs a junction table.

[MEM_SAVE short] {"type":"thought","content":"Schema decisions: tasks need status enum (todo/in_progress/done), priority enum (low/medium/high), due_date. task_tags junction table (task_id FK, tag_id FK). Index on tasks(project_id, status) for common query.","priority":"high","why":"Column and index design decisions."}
[SCHEDULE 20]

Design complete. Delivering schema to user, archiving the work, cleaning short-term memory.

[SEND_MESSAGE] {"text":"Here's the PostgreSQL schema:

CREATE TYPE task_status AS ENUM ('todo', 'in_progress', 'done');
CREATE TYPE task_priority AS ENUM ('low', 'medium', 'high');

CREATE TABLE users (id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(), email TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL, name TEXT NOT NULL, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW());
CREATE TABLE projects (id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(), owner_id UUID REFERENCES users(id), name TEXT NOT NULL, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW());
CREATE TABLE tasks (id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(), project_id UUID REFERENCES projects(id), assignee_id UUID REFERENCES users(id), title TEXT NOT NULL, status task_status DEFAULT 'todo', priority task_priority DEFAULT 'medium', due_date DATE, created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW());
CREATE TABLE tags (id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(), name TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE task_tags (task_id UUID REFERENCES tasks(id) ON DELETE CASCADE, tag_id UUID REFERENCES tags(id), PRIMARY KEY (task_id, tag_id));
CREATE INDEX ON tasks(project_id, status);

Want me to add soft delete, audit columns, or full-text search?","why":"Task complete after 3 cycles of design reasoning."}
[MEM_SAVE long] {"type":"insight","content":"Task management schema: users, projects, tasks (status/priority enums), tags, task_tags junction. Composite index on (project_id, status). UUID primary keys.","tags":"database,postgresql,schema,task_management","why":"Archiving completed design work."}
